WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.114
Authorization depends on trustworthy
context: subject identifier,

2
00:00:04.194 --> 00:00:08.298
tenant or organization, roles or
permissions, resource identity, ownership,

3
00:00:08.378 --> 00:00:10.339
current state, and requested action.

4
00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:13.298
If a required value is missing or cannot
be established,

5
00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:16.196
the application lacks evidence for an
allow decision.

6
00:00:16.276 --> 00:00:20.135
Filling that gap with an administrative
role, unrestricted tenant scope,

7
00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:22.849
or a default owner creates authority from
absence.

8
00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:27.033
Build a validated AuthContext at a clear
authentication boundary and pass it to

9
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:28.767
protected service operations.

10
00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:33.197
Required fields should be required in the
service interface rather than repeatedly

11
00:00:33.277 --> 00:00:35.359
read from optional request properties.

12
00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:39.972
Runtime construction still matters because
a TypeScript interface cannot populate or

13
00:00:40.052 --> 00:00:40.971
verify a request.

14
00:00:41.051 --> 00:00:44.053
Reject malformed claims, unresolved tenant
membership,

15
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:47.012
and inconsistent identity data before
authorization.

16
00:00:47.092 --> 00:00:51.196
Roles and tenant membership must come from
a trusted identity or policy source,

17
00:00:51.276 --> 00:00:54.094
not request-body or query fields chosen by
the client.

18
00:00:54.174 --> 00:00:56.502
Middleware ordering is part of the
boundary.

19
00:00:56.582 --> 00:00:59.216
If authentication middleware did not run,
failed,

20
00:00:59.296 --> 00:01:02.359
or did not establish the fields a
protected route requires,

21
00:01:02.439 --> 00:01:06.482
the handler must not continue as though it
received a valid anonymous context.

22
00:01:06.562 --> 00:01:10.054
Optional authentication can be correct for
public functionality,

23
00:01:10.134 --> 00:01:13.809
but each operation should declare whether
anonymous use is supported.

24
00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:18.238
A protected mutation should not infer that
choice from a missing req.user property.

25
00:01:18.318 --> 00:01:20.524
Resource context deserves the same rigor.

26
00:01:20.604 --> 00:01:24.769
If policy depends on an object's tenant,
owner, sensitivity, or workflow state,

27
00:01:24.849 --> 00:01:27.667
load those attributes through a trusted,
scoped path.

28
00:01:27.747 --> 00:01:29.646
A missing record may map to not found,

29
00:01:29.726 --> 00:01:33.646
while a database failure may map to a
service error; neither supports allow.

30
00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:36.361
Bind the decision to the specific subject,
action,

31
00:01:36.441 --> 00:01:40.484
and resource used by the side effect so
that stale or mismatched context cannot

32
00:01:40.564 --> 00:01:42.340
authorize a different operation.
